+# Why the Approve button turns into one giant spinner
+
+## What the class combo actually does
+
+The bug is `className="btn ... loading"` — putting `loading` **directly on the button**.
+
+In DaisyUI, `loading` is **not** a button modifier. It's a standalone
+component class that turns *the element it's on* into a spinner. Its CSS is
+roughly:
+
+```css
+.loading {
+  pointer-events: none;
+  display: inline-block;
+  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;                 /* forces a square */
+  width: 1.5rem;                       /* loading-md default size */
+  background-color: currentColor;      /* fill = text color */
+  mask-repeat: no-repeat;
+  mask-position: center;
+  mask-size: 100%;                     /* mask stretched over the whole box */
+  mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml,<the spinner svg>");
+}
+```
+
+The key is `mask-image` + `mask-size: 100%` + `background-color: currentColor`.
+When those land on the `<button>` itself:
+
+- The button's whole box is painted with `currentColor` and then **masked to
+  the spinner silhouette**. A CSS mask clips the element *and all its
+  children*, so the "Approving..." text node is masked away too — that's why
+  the **label never shows**. It's not hidden or `display:none`; it's clipped
+  out by the mask.
+- `mask-size: 100%` makes the spinner stretch to the full button box, so it
+  reads as **one big spinner filling the whole button** instead of a small
+  glyph.
+- `aspect-ratio: 1 / 1` + `width: 1.5rem` fight the button's normal
+  padding/auto width, so the button **resizes/reshapes** the moment it goes
+  pending — that's the **layout jump**. Same on desktop and mobile because
+  it's pure CSS geometry, not a breakpoint issue.
+
+So DaisyUI is doing exactly what `.loading` is designed to do — it just
+should never be applied to the button; it should be applied to a small child
+element *next to* the text.
+
+(Historical note: in old DaisyUI v2/v3 `.btn.loading` *was* a supported
+button state. It was removed in v4 — `loading` is now only the standalone
+component. Copy-pasting the old pattern onto a v4 project produces exactly
+this symptom.)
+
+## The corrected button — idiomatic DaisyUI v4 / Scaffold-ETH 2
+
+Keep `btn btn-primary` clean. Render a **small** spinner as a child element
+(`loading loading-spinner loading-sm`) *beside* the visible label:
+
+```tsx
+<button className="btn btn-primary" disabled={isPending}>
+  {isPending && <span className="loading loading-spinner loading-sm"></span>}
+  {isPending ? "Approving..." : "Approve"}
+</button>
+```
+
+Why this is the right pattern:
+
+- The spinner is its own `<span>`, so the mask only clips the span — the
+  "Approving..." text stays fully visible next to it.
+- `loading-spinner` picks the spinner style; `loading-sm` keeps it inline-sized
+  instead of the 1.5rem default, so it sits neatly next to text.
+- `.btn` already sets `display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem`,
+  so the spinner and label are auto-aligned and spaced — no extra flex/margin
+  wrappers needed.
+- The spinner inherits `currentColor`, so on `btn-primary` it matches the
+  label color automatically.
+- Button width grows only by the small spinner's width, so there's no jarring
+  layout jump.
+
+If you want to avoid even the small width shift, reserve the space by always
+rendering the spinner and toggling visibility instead:
+
+```tsx
+<button className="btn btn-primary" disabled={isPending}>
+  <span className={`loading loading-spinner loading-sm ${isPending ? "" : "invisible"}`}></span>
+  {isPending ? "Approving..." : "Approve"}
+</button>
+```

